[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124242,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Verruca plantaris is an HPV-induced benign epidermal squamoproliferative lesion affecting the soles or palms.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:04:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124243,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:04:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124244,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>1E80.1 Plantar warts</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:04:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124245,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> plantar wart; palmoplantar wart; plantar verruca.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> myrmecia.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:04:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124246,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:04:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124247,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The soles and palms are the principal sites of involvement.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:04:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124248,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The lesions can be single or multiple, and they are often painful. The myrmecial form (associated with HPV1) presents as a hard, slightly elevated keratotic papule or nodule on the plantar or palmar surface. The more superficial mosaic form (associated with HPV4 and HPV2) exhibits confluent growth and is resistant to therapy {22131115; 6299203}. Black dots reflect intralesional vascular thrombosis. Dermoscopy is a useful diagnostic adjunct and tool for assessing response to treatment {34538428}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:04:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124249,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Children are affected more frequently than adults, with a reported prevalence of 1.8&ndash;20% {10770027; 19438464}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:04:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124250,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>HPV1, HPV2, and HPV4 usually predominate {22131115; 6299203}. HPV27 and HPV57 have been recorded in cases from Spain and California {27736001; 24725033}. HPV60 has been linked to plantar warts and epidermal cysts {7979443}. Primary hyperhidrosis and visits to swimming pools and communal showers are risk factors {19395123; 23932731; 7852935}. Patients with HIV may have larger and more numerous lesions {9770936}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:04:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124251,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See the <em>Pathogenesis</em> subsection of the <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/64/10\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Verruca vulgaris</em> section .</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:04:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124252,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:04:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124253,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>The lesions are characterized by marked hyperkeratosis, acanthosis, hypergranulosis, and koilocytosis {22131115}. Papillomatosis is usually mild. The myrmecial (ant-hill) form shows endophytic growth and vacuolated keratinocytes containing coarse eosinophilic keratohyalin granules. Regressing lesions exhibit haemorrhage, necrosis, thrombosis, and a mixed inflammatory infiltrate {7059201}.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Callosities occur on pressure points and are also endophytic and hyperkeratotic, but they lack koilocytes and coarse keratohyalin granules {27023088}. Molluscum contagiosum also displays endophytic growth but has basophilic intracytoplasmic inclusions rather than heterogeneous eosinophilic keratohyalin granules {20398595}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:05:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 775,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124254,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 775,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:04:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 833,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 776,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124255,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 776,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Routine HPV subtyping is not indicated.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:04:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 836,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 779,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124256,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 779,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> papillomatosis; parakeratotic columns; coarse eosinophilic keratohyalin granules; koilocytosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:04:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 834,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 777,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124257,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 777,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:04:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 835,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 778,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124258,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 11,
        "headingId": 778,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Verruca plantaris often regresses spontaneously. Lesions in children typically resolve more rapidly {22131115}. Verrucous carcinoma is a very rare complication {6168555}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:04:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]